`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
Two brittle patterns dominate this plan's testability: (1) substring-presence tests that verify phrase literals but not semantic content, and (2) acceptance criteria that lock in a rule while the Risks/Open list still frames the same rule as undecided. Both let a vacuous contract pass every gate.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

Verdict: MAJOR

Findings:
- [MAJOR] Universal placement rule is lexically enforced but semantically undefined — Line 150 Deliverable and line 253 Acceptance mandate a "universal repo-vs-bulk-artifact-store placement rule," and test `test_tier1_contract_requires_repo_vs_bulk_artifact_store_rule` (line 234) verifies only that "universal rule plus example language present." `grep` of `docs/standards/` shows zero prior occurrences of "bulk-artifact-store"; this is a term the plan introduces without defining. The contract author can satisfy every stated test by pasting the phrase into a sentence, without ever specifying which classes of artifact belong in a repo versus a bulk store. There is no acceptance criterion requiring the contract to enumerate what the rule means (e.g., size thresholds, mutability, provenance rules, examples of repo-kept vs. store-bound artifacts). This is the core durable rule of the contract and the plan's test is a substring check.
- [MAJOR] Open question on line 296 contradicts the locked acceptance criteria — Line 296 is framed as "Open: Should the contract state the universal policy as repo-vs-bulk-artifact-store with `/mnt/ace/data` documented as the current workspace-hub implementation example?" but line 253 already mandates exactly that wording as an acceptance criterion and line 234 bakes it into a TDD test. A plan cannot simultaneously treat a decision as open and as an acceptance gate — if it's open, the criteria should be conditional; if it's locked, the Open should be resolved and deleted. Same contradiction affects line 298 ("machine-readable registry" filename standardization), which is listed as Open while line 231 already enforces "one canonical machine-readable registry per repo" as a test requirement.
- [MINOR] Daily-freshness "same section" proximity is claimed but not enforced — Pseudocode line 174 and acceptance line 256 require the cadence phrase and #2465 follow-through linkage "in the same freshness section," but test `test_tier1_contract_requires_daily_freshness_cadence` (line 239) asks only for "exact cadence wording present" and the #2465 test is a separate check (line 238). Two independent substring tests cannot verify section co-location. A contract that puts "daily freshness review" in a section header and "every 24 hours" in a distant footnote, with "#2465" in a different part of the doc altogether, would pass every test while violating the stated intent.
- [MINOR] Scorecard negative-authority sentence is path-literal and will decay — Acceptance line 257 and test line 240 hardcode the exact string `MUST NOT treat docs/reports/2026-04-22-tier-1-indexing-scorecard.md as required canonical authority`. Since #2465 is explicitly a daily-freshness automation issue that will produce new dated scorecards, the prohibition binds to one stale filename and says nothing about future scorecards of the same class. The rule should target the artifact class ("any tier-1 indexing scorecard under docs/reports/") — or at minimum be paired with a forward-looking clause — otherwise it becomes archaeological verbiage within weeks.
- [MINOR] Checklist field names are required but field values are untyped — Acceptance line 260 and test line 243 enforce presence of `repo_name`, `operator_map_status`, `registry_status`, `data_placement_status`, `evidence_source`. Nothing constrains what values those fields may take. A checklist with `registry_status: TBD` or `evidence_source: see somewhere else` for every repo would pass. At minimum the plan should specify the enum domain (e.g., `present | partial | missing`) and the shape of `evidence_source` (path or URL or SHA), or spawn a follow-up test that validates field values.
- [MINOR] Artifact Map row 141 is vestigial — `Optional script/docs drift follow-up only (not in this issue) | scripts/ / child issue #2465` is listed among deliverable artifacts while simultaneously declaring itself out of scope. That row should be deleted from the Artifact Map and, if retained, moved to Risks/Open as a scope-boundary note; its current placement invites a reviewer to believe the plan touches `scripts/`.
- [MINOR] Legacy-retirement "concrete banned-pattern signatures" has no floor — Test line 236 requires "concrete signatures present" but does not quantify how many, nor whether they must include specific categories (literal filenames, path fragments, and reference blocks as the pseudocode line 171-172 suggests). A contract that enumerates one filename satisfies "concrete signatures" as a plural and passes. The enforcement is one notch stronger than the prior "token example" but still a floor of one, effectively.
- [MINOR] Stale review-state language is cleaned but the "Current draft state" blurb blurs authority — Line 280-281 is correct that this draft is pending re-review, but the preceding table (lines 274-278) presents the historical verdicts without a per-row "Superseded by patch on line 282-287" annotation. A future reader skimming the table alone could mistake the Claude/Codex/Gemini MAJOR verdicts as current. Consider adding a "Status: superseded 2026-04-22" column or move the table under a clearly collapsed "Historical" heading.

Checks performed:
- Read the full plan at docs/plans/2026-04-22-issue-2460-tier1-indexing-and-code-placement-contract.md (lines 1-305)
- Verified existence of the four standards/reports the plan cites (DATA_PLACEMENT, CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT, FILE_STRUCTURE_TAXONOMY, scorecard) — all present
- Grepped `docs/standards/` for "bulk-artifact-store" / "bulk artifact store" — zero prior definitions, confirming the term is new and undefined
- Cross-checked each rerun focus area (stale language, universal placement rule, freshness pass condition, scorecard negative-authority, checklist fields) against the Deliverable, Pseudocode, TDD test list, and Acceptance Criteria for consistency
- Mapped each acceptance criterion to its corresponding TDD test to detect lexical-vs-semantic gaps
- Scanned Risks/Open Questions for contradictions with locked acceptance criteria
- Did not verify review-artifact files under `scripts/review/results/` (out of rerun focus)
- Did not audit test implementations (none exist yet — this is pre-implementation)
